---
import Docs from "~/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import { faqPageSchema, howToSchema } from "~/lib/structured-data";
import { SITE_URL } from "~/lib/og";
import { PROVIDERS, CATEGORIES } from "~/lib/providers";

const pageUrl = `${SITE_URL}/docs/recommend/`;
const providerCount = PROVIDERS.length;
const categoryCount = CATEGORIES.length;

const howTo = howToSchema({
  name: "Recommend and apply a Stack recipe",
  description:
    "Retrieve catalog candidates, save a recipe, review its provider effects and inspect setup and optional wiring results.",
  url: pageUrl,
  steps: [
    {
      name: "Describe what you're building",
      text: "Run `stack recommend \"<query>\" --save` to rank providers and freeze a recipe to .stack/recipes/<id>.toml.",
      url: `${pageUrl}#quick-start`,
    },
    {
      name: "Apply the recipe",
      text: "Run `stack apply <recipe-id>` after reviewing the saved recipe. It attempts provider setup and a names-only Phantom vault check plus webhook stubs; inspect partial results.",
      url: `${pageUrl}#stack-apply`,
    },
  ],
});

const faq = faqPageSchema(
  [
    {
      question: "Can I use my own LLM?",
      answer:
        "STACK_LM_URL can select an OpenAI-compatible endpoint for optional synthesis. Default addresses are local LM Studio/Ollama; review any endpoint override and its authentication/privacy requirements before use.",
    },
    {
      question: "Does anything call Anthropic or OpenAI?",
      answer:
        "Default recommendation is retrieval-only. --synth can contact the configured HTTP endpoint; local defaults do not guarantee every override is local. The external coding agent has its own provider and privacy settings.",
    },
    {
      question: "What if the SLM isn't running?",
      answer:
        "`--synth` silently falls back to retrieval-only output. BM25 scoring and recipe freezing still work with no LLM at all.",
    },
  ],
  pageUrl,
);
---

<Docs
  title="AI recommend + apply"
  description="Retrieve catalog matches, save a recipe and review provider setup. Optional wiring attempts are not accepted production rotation."
  eyebrow="Getting started"
  extraSchemas={[howTo, faq]}
>
  <h2>Why this exists</h2>
  <p>
    Picking the right providers is the first friction point in a new project.
    Stack's catalog has <strong>{providerCount} curated providers</strong> across {categoryCount}
    categories — too many to hold in your head, too few to outsource to a
    generic LLM. <code>stack recommend</code> closes the gap: free-text in,
    ranked providers out, frozen to a reproducible Recipe that{" "}
    <code>stack apply</code> can replay.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout-info">
    <strong>Retrieval-first, not LLM-first.</strong> Ranking runs through a
    zero-dep BM25 + IDF scorer over the curated catalog. Claude Code (via MCP)
    or a local SLM can rewrite rationales — but the shortlist is deterministic
    and offline.
  </div>

  <h2 id="quick-start">Quick start</h2>
  <p>Save a recommendation, review it, then authorize setup in the intended directory:</p>

  <Code
    code={`stack recommend "B2B SaaS with auth, AI, and payments" --save
# → .stack/recipes/b2b-saas-with-auth-ai-and-paymen.toml

stack apply b2b-saas-with-auth-ai-and-paymen`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <h2>Two modes</h2>
  <p>
    Default recommendation is retrieval-only. Optional synthesis can contact
    the configured HTTP endpoint; a local default does not constrain overrides.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 28%">Mode</th>
        <th style="width: 32%">When it fires</th>
        <th>What does the reasoning</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Claude Code (MCP)</strong></td>
        <td>
          You're in a Claude / Cursor / Windsurf session and the agent calls{" "}
          <code>stack_recommend</code>.
        </td>
        <td>
          Claude consumes Stack's retrieval output as grounded context and
          may write its own rationale. The MCP wrapper returns CLI text; the
          external agent has its own provider settings.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Standalone CLI (local SLM)</strong></td>
        <td>
          You run <code>stack recommend --synth</code> in a terminal, no agent
          attached.
        </td>
        <td>
          Stack speaks OpenAI-compatible HTTP to LM Studio (<code>:1234</code>){" "}
          or Ollama (<code>:11434</code>). Missing endpoint? Falls back to
          retrieval-only output — command still exits 0.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>
    <code>stack recommend</code>
  </h2>
  <p>Rank providers against a free-text description. Flags compose.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 20%">Flag</th>
        <th style="width: 12%">Type</th>
        <th>What it does</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--save</code></td>
        <td class="text-ink-400 mono text-[12px]">boolean</td>
        <td>
          Freeze the result as <code>.stack/recipes/&lt;id&gt;.toml</code>. The
          id is slugified from the query. Feed it to <code>stack apply</code>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--synth</code></td>
        <td class="text-ink-400 mono text-[12px]">boolean</td>
        <td>
          Call the configured HTTP endpoint for model-authored rationales. Defaults are local; endpoint overrides may be remote. Inference failure can fall back to retrieval-only output.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--json</code></td>
        <td class="text-ink-400 mono text-[12px]">boolean</td>
        <td>
          CLI JSON output; the MCP wrapper carries this inside text content.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--k</code></td>
        <td class="text-ink-400 mono text-[12px]">number</td>
        <td>
          Max hits to return. Default <code>6</code>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--category</code></td>
        <td class="text-ink-400 mono text-[12px]">string</td>
        <td>
          Restrict to one category: <code>Database</code>, <code>Deploy</code>,{" "}
          <code>Cloud</code>, <code>AI</code>, <code>Analytics</code>,{" "}
          <code>Observability</code>, <code>FeatureFlags</code>, <code>Errors</code>, <code>Payments</code>, <code>Code</code>,{" "}
          <code>Tickets</code>, <code>Email</code>, <code>Auth</code>.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Default output</h3>
  <p>Illustrative output and scores; actual matches depend on the current catalog. Retrieval scores are not measured provider quality.</p>
  <Code
    code={`$ stack recommend "B2B SaaS with auth, AI, and payments"

┌   stack recommend
  query: B2B SaaS with auth, AI, and payments

  ● Clerk  Auth  (4.13)
    Drop-in auth + users. Secret key stored in Phantom.
    add with: stack add clerk

  ● Stripe  Payments  (3.70)
    Billing, subscriptions, tax. Restricted / test-mode secret key stored in Phantom.
    add with: stack add stripe

  ● xAI  AI  (2.90)
    Grok + tool use. Key verified on paste.
    add with: stack add xai

  ● Sentry  Errors  (2.48)
    Error + performance tracking. Attaches to an existing project and reads its DSN when available.
    add with: stack add sentry

  ● OpenAI  AI  (2.23)
    GPT, Realtime, embeddings. Key verified against /v1/models on paste.
    add with: stack add openai

  ● Anthropic  AI  (2.23)
    Claude models + MCP. Key verified against the Messages API on paste.
    add with: stack add anthropic

  Top pick per category: Auth→clerk, Payments→stripe, AI→xai, Errors→sentry.
  No matches surfaced for: Database, Deploy, Cloud, Analytics, Code, Tickets, Email.
  Apply a chosen set with: stack add clerk && stack add stripe && stack add xai (one command per service).

  Tip: rerun with \`--save\` to freeze as a recipe for \`stack apply\`.
│
└  Tell Claude or run \`stack add <name>\` to continue.`}
    lang="text"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <h3><code>--save</code> — freeze to a Recipe</h3>
  <Code
    code={`$ stack recommend "B2B SaaS with auth" --save

  ● Clerk  Auth  (4.13)
  ● Sentry  Errors  (2.48)
  ● Supabase  Database  (2.16)
  ● Firebase  Database  (2.16)
  ● Turso  Database  (0.79)

  Saved recipe: b2b-saas-with-auth   (/path/to/.stack/recipes/b2b-saas-with-auth.toml)
  Apply with:  stack apply b2b-saas-with-auth`}
    lang="text"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <h3><code>--synth</code> — local-SLM rationales</h3>
  <p>
    With LM Studio or Ollama running, each hit gains a{" "}
    <code>why:</code> line authored by the model. Without either, the command
    can fall back to retrieval-only output. Other I/O or setup errors remain possible. Illustrative synthesis output:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`$ stack recommend "analytics + error tracking for a Next.js app" --synth

  ● PostHog  Analytics  (5.01)
    Product analytics + session replay. Personal API key stored in Phantom.
    why: PostHog gives you funnels, flags, and replay for a Next.js app in one SDK.
    add with: stack add posthog

  ● Sentry  Errors  (4.77)
    Error + performance tracking. Attaches to an existing project and reads its DSN when available.
    why: Sentry captures unhandled exceptions and Web Vitals without extra glue.
    add with: stack add sentry`}
    lang="text"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <h3><code>--json</code> — machine-readable</h3>
  <p>
    This is illustrative CLI JSON, not the outer MCP text envelope. The
    example fields and scores are not a live acceptance receipt:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`{
  "query": "B2B SaaS with auth and payments",
  "hits": [
    {
      "name": "clerk",
      "displayName": "Clerk",
      "category": "Auth",
      "authKind": "api_key",
      "secrets": ["CLERK_SECRET_KEY"],
      "blurb": "Drop-in auth + users. Secret key stored in Phantom.",
      "score": 4.125,
      "matched": ["auth"]
    },
    {
      "name": "stripe",
      "displayName": "Stripe",
      "category": "Payments",
      "authKind": "api_key",
      "secrets": ["STRIPE_SECRET_KEY"],
      "blurb": "Billing, subscriptions, tax. …",
      "score": 3.697,
      "matched": ["payments"]
    }
  ],
  "byCategory": { "Auth": [{ "name": "clerk", "score": 4.125 }], "…": [] },
  "guidance": "Top pick per category: Auth→clerk, Payments→stripe. …"
}`}
    lang="json"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <h3><code>--k</code> — cap the hits</h3>
  <Code
    code={`$ stack recommend "postgres database" --k 3

  ● Neon  Database  (5.76)
    Serverless Postgres. Creates a project and pools the connection string.
  ● Supabase  Database  (5.49)
    Postgres + Auth + Storage. Full upstream provisioning via the Management API.
  ● Turso  Database  (4.35)
    Edge SQLite (libSQL). Creates a database in your default org.`}
    lang="text"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <h3><code>--category</code> — restrict to one category</h3>
  <Code
    code={`$ stack recommend "serverless" --category Database

  ● Neon  Database  (5.76)
    Serverless Postgres. Creates a project and pools the connection string.
  ● Turso  Database  (2.18)
    Edge SQLite (libSQL). Creates a database in your default org.`}
    lang="text"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <h2 id="stack-apply">
    <code>stack apply</code>
  </h2>
  <p>
    Replay a saved Recipe through the existing <code>stack add</code> pipeline,
    then check which declared secrets are in the Phantom vault. Setup may
    create or attach resources and write credentials/config; the vault check
    only reads names, and webhook stubs are not verified handlers.
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`# Illustrative replay; inspect actual setup and optional wiring results
$ stack apply b2b-saas-with-auth

  recipe   b2b-saas-with-auth
  query    B2B SaaS with auth
  providers clerk, sentry, supabase

  › stack add clerk
  › stack add sentry
  › stack add supabase

  secrets in vault 7 · missing 1 · webhooks 2
  (not in Phantom: SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN — store with \`phantom add <NAME>\`)

  › stack doctor
  ✓ b2b-saas-with-auth applied.`}
    lang="text"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 20%">Flag</th>
        <th>What it does</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--noWire</code></td>
        <td>
          Skip the Phantom-wire layer — pure <code>stack add</code> replay, no
          vault check or webhook stubs. Provider setup and credential/config writes
          still occur; this flag is not a dry run.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--noRollback</code></td>
        <td>
          Skip the multi-service cleanup attempt after partial failure.
          Existing provider resources, credentials or partial writes may remain;
          this flag is not a dry run or a guarantee about upstream deletion.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>What gets wired</h3>
  <p>After setup succeeds for an entry, the default flow can attempt these steps unless <code>--noWire</code> is set:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Phantom vault check</strong> — reads names from
      <code>phantom list --json</code> (never values) and lists each declared
      secret as in the vault or missing. It never writes to the vault; store
      missing values with <code>phantom add &lt;NAME&gt;</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Webhook stubs</strong> — scaffolded verification + handler files
      for selected providers in the helper: <code>stripe</code>, <code>clerk</code>, <code>supabase</code>,{" "}
      <code>github</code>. Implement and verify handlers in the app before using them; generated
      comments are not signature verification.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Inline <code>stack doctor</code></strong> — runs at the end so
      you can inspect available checks. It is informational and not an
      all-services-success gate for <code>apply</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Inspect before replay.</strong> Existing services can be skipped,
    but partial provider, credential or optional wiring effects may remain.
    Re-running <code>stack apply</code> is not a universal safe-retry guarantee.
  </div>

  <h2>MCP tools</h2>
  <p>
    Both commands are exposed as MCP tools so Claude Code (and any MCP-speaking
    editor) can request these operations when its actual tools and project
    context are configured. See the full{" "}
    <a href="/docs/mcp/">MCP integration docs</a> for setup.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose mt-4 panel rounded-xl p-5">
    <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3 flex-wrap mb-2">
      <code class="mono text-magenta-200 text-[14px] font-semibold">stack_recommend</code>
      <code class="mono text-[11.5px] text-ink-500">stack recommend …</code>
    </div>
    <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      Retrieval-only. Returns ranked providers with scores, matched terms, and
      per-category top picks. Pass <code>save: true</code> to also freeze a
      Recipe to disk so you can follow up with <code>stack_apply</code>.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th style="width: 22%">Input</th>
          <th style="width: 14%">Type</th>
          <th>Description</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <code class="mono text-[12px]">query</code>
            <span class="ml-1.5 text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-magenta-400 font-semibold">req</span>
          </td>
          <td class="text-ink-400 mono text-[12px]">string</td>
          <td class="text-[13px]">Free-text description of the project.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code class="mono text-[12px]">k</code></td>
          <td class="text-ink-400 mono text-[12px]">number</td>
          <td class="text-[13px]">Max results (default 6).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code class="mono text-[12px]">category</code></td>
          <td class="text-ink-400 mono text-[12px]">string</td>
          <td class="text-[13px]">Restrict to one category.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code class="mono text-[12px]">save</code></td>
          <td class="text-ink-400 mono text-[12px]">boolean</td>
          <td class="text-[13px]">
            Freeze to <code>.stack/recipes/&lt;id&gt;.toml</code>; returns the
            recipe id.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="not-prose mt-4 panel rounded-xl p-5">
    <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3 flex-wrap mb-2">
      <code class="mono text-magenta-200 text-[14px] font-semibold">stack_apply</code>
      <code class="mono text-[11.5px] text-ink-500">stack apply …</code>
    </div>
    <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      Replay a saved Recipe. Runs <code>stack add</code> for each provider,
      then checks the Phantom vault and writes webhook stubs. The returned text
      can include failures; setup and cleanup are not atomic.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th style="width: 22%">Input</th>
          <th style="width: 14%">Type</th>
          <th>Description</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <code class="mono text-[12px]">recipe_id</code>
            <span class="ml-1.5 text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-magenta-400 font-semibold">req</span>
          </td>
          <td class="text-ink-400 mono text-[12px]">string</td>
          <td class="text-[13px]">
            Recipe id (filename stem in <code>.stack/recipes/</code>).
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code class="mono text-[12px]">no_wire</code></td>
          <td class="text-ink-400 mono text-[12px]">boolean</td>
          <td class="text-[13px]">Skip the Phantom vault check and webhook stubs.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>In-session example</h3>
  <p>
    Illustrative conversation, not a recorded customer or provider outcome:
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`You > Build me a SaaS — auth, payments, error tracking, Postgres.

Claude > I'll pick the providers first and freeze a recipe so you can
         replay this.
         (calls stack_recommend { query: "SaaS with auth, payments,
          error tracking, and Postgres", save: true })

         Top picks: clerk (Auth), stripe (Payments), sentry (Errors),
         neon (Database). Saved as recipe "saas-with-auth-payments-error".

         Want me to apply it now? This runs \`stack add\` for each and
         checks which secrets are already in Phantom.

You > Yes.

Claude > (calls stack_apply { recipe_id: "saas-with-auth-payments-error" })

         Setup report: inspect added, skipped and failed entries.
         Optional wiring counts and doctor checks need separate verification.`}
    lang="text"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <h2>Recipe file format</h2>
  <p>
    Recipes are plain TOML under <code>.stack/recipes/&lt;id&gt;.toml</code>.
    Recipes can include the original query and freeform rationales. Review
    them before sharing; dedicated credential persistence uses
    <a href="/docs/phantom/">Phantom</a>.
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`id = "b2b-saas-with-auth"
query = "B2B SaaS with auth"
createdAt = "2026-04-19T02:22:44.810Z"
guidance = "Top pick per category: Auth→clerk, Errors→sentry, Database→supabase."

[[providers]]
name = "clerk"
rationale = "auth"

[[providers]]
name = "sentry"
rationale = "auth"

[[providers]]
name = "supabase"
rationale = "auth"`}
    lang="toml"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    The <code>id</code> is the first ~40 chars of the query, slugified. The{" "}
    <code>rationale</code> starts as the matched terms from retrieval and gets
    may be updated when an authorized synthesis result is saved. An agent
    writing prose externally does not itself rewrite the stored recipe.
  </p>

  <h2>How retrieval works</h2>
  <p>
    Zero-dep BM25 + IDF scorer over the curated provider catalog, with a small
    table of hand-authored synonyms (<code>db</code> → <code>database</code>,{" "}
    <code>billing</code> → <code>payments</code>, <code>auth</code> →{" "}
    <code>users</code>, …) so natural-language queries map to the terms we
    actually index. Scoring is deterministic — same query, same ranking. The
    catalog itself lives in <code>packages/core/src/catalog.ts</code> and is
    the same source <a href="/docs/providers/">the providers page</a> renders
    from.
  </p>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>

  <h3>Can I use my own LLM?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes. Set <code>STACK_LM_URL</code> to any OpenAI-compatible endpoint. Stack
    talks plain HTTP to LM Studio (<code>localhost:1234</code>) and Ollama
    (<code>localhost:11434</code>) by default. An override may contact another
    host; verify its access and privacy requirements.
  </p>

  <h3>Does anything call Anthropic or OpenAI?</h3>
  <p>
    Default retrieval uses no model. <strong>Optional synthesis</strong> can
    contact a configured endpoint, while <strong>the external coding agent</strong>
    has its own provider settings. Absence of an SDK is not a no-network proof.
  </p>

  <h3>What if the SLM isn't running?</h3>
  <p>
    <code>--synth</code> silently falls back to retrieval-only output. The
    ranking remains retrieval-based, and <code>--save</code> can persist the
    recipe. Endpoint fallback does not rule out other I/O errors or guarantee
    an exit code of <code>0</code>.
  </p>

